			  LICENSE AGREEMENT
		 BETWEEN THE PLASMA SIMULATION CENTER
			AND ITER ORGANIZATION


Plasma Simulation Center, having its seat at Lihachevskoe shosse 10
building 2, Dolgoprudniy, Moscow region, Russia, (hereinafter referred
to as "the Licensor"), which is a representative of Dr. Victor Lukash
and Dr. Rustam Khayrutdinov (hereinafter referred to as "DINA-team")
and is represented for the purpose of signing this Agreement by its
duly authorised representative Mr. Sergey Konovalov, Director General;

on the one part,

The ITER International Fusion Energy Organization (hereinafter
referred to as "the ITER Organization"), (hereinafter referred to as
"the Licensee"), represented for the purposes of signing this
Agreement by David Campbell, Director of Plasma Operation and Chair of
the Intellectual Property Board, duly delegated by the Director
General of the ITER Organization by delegation of authority;

on the other part,

Hereinafter referred to collectively as the "Parties" and individually
as the "Party"

			     HAVE AGREED


		       ARTICLE 1 - DEFINITIONS

"Computer" means an electronic device that accepts information in
digital or similar form and manipulates it for specific results based
on a sequence of instructions.

"Software" means the DINA scenario code and any modification or
updated versions thereof. The Software also means all the contents of
the files (provided either by electronic download, on physical media
or any other method of distribution), disk(s), CD-ROM(s) or other
media being object of this Agreement.

"Software Package" means the Software and support materials licensed
by the Licensor and any developments thereof by the Licensor,
including but not limited to manuals, flow charts and specifications
relating to the Software.

"Technical Support" means the services provided by the DINA-team for
the configuration of the Software on the computers of the
Licensee. Technical support may include enhancement or development
services on the code under additional agreement.

"Trade secret" means all confidential and sensitive information of the
Licensor transmitted to the Licensee in any format and by any means in
the course of this Agreement.

"Source Code" means statements of sequence written in a computer
programming language in human readable form which is used as an input
to the process that produces a machine executable action or software
product. In the context of this Agreement, Source Code refers to the
source code of the Software. Source Code includes, without limitation:
 a) source codes for the executable packages of a software product,
    application or tool;
 b) source codes for the modules forming part of the executable
    package of a software product, application or tool;
 c) definition files associated with the source code;
 d) scripts used to compile the executable package; and
 e) scripts used to install the executable package.

"Derivative Work" means the incremental work constituting
enhancements, improvements, and developments, together with interfaces
and modifications to the Software itself or to the Source Code, in
whole or in part, manuals, specifications, and related written
material, as well as secondary works deriving directly from the
modification of the Software.

"Use" or "Used" in connection with the Software means storing any
portion of the Source Code on a machine, and/or transmitting any
portion of the Source Code to a machine for processing and/or
compiling, executing or interpreting any instructions contained in the
Source Code, and/or displaying any portion of the Source Code in
connection with the processing of such instructions.

"License" means the rights granted by the Licensor to the Licensee to
Use the Software in accordance with the present Agreement.

"Effective Date" means the date of signature of this Agreement by the
last of the Parties.

"ITER Member States" means the People's Republic of China, the
European Atomic Energy Community, the Republic of India, Japan, the
Republic of Korea, the Russian Federation and the United States of
America.


			  ARTICLE 2 - SCOPE

1. The Licensor grants the Licensee a perpetual, irrevocable
non-exclusive, non-transferable and royalty-free License to access the
Source Code and to Use and modify the Software and the Software
Package for non-commercial research purposes including without
limitation, for the design, construction, operation, maintenance and
repair of the ITER facilities.

2. The Licensee shall have the right to grant sub-licenses to access
the Source Code and to Use and modify the Software and the Software
Package to third parties within the ITER Member States on the same
terms as the terms of this License Agreement. Save as otherwise
provided in this License Agreement, the Licensee may not rent, lease,
sublicense, or trade the Software and the Software Package to any
third party, or assign, transfer or grant any kind of rights regarding
the Software and the Software Package or any portions thereof.

3. Save as otherwise provided under this License Agreement, the
Licensee may not disclose, make available, distribute, assign or
transfer the Source Code, or any portions thereof, to any third party
without prior express written authorisation of the Licensor.

4.  The Licensee may modify and create Derivative Works based on the
Source Code, Software and the Software Package, in whole or in part,
and integrate the modified Source Code and Software within the
platform of the Licensee for the research purposes.


			ARTICLE 3 - OWNERSHIP

1. The Source Code, the Software and the Software Package remain under
the ownership of the Licensor, and the Licensor will continue to
operate the Software independently. As such, the Licensee does not
acquire any proprietary right, title or interest in the Source Code,
the Software, the Software Package save as provided under this License
Agreement.

2. The Licensee must at all times acknowledge and duly reference the
Software, mention that it has been developed by DINA-team.

3. No right other than what is expressly granted herein may be deemed
as granted by implication.

4. Any Derivative Work developed by the Licensee shall be the
exclusive property of the Licensee.


			 ARTICLE 4 - DURATION

1. This Agreement enters into force at the effective date, and shall
stay valid for either the duration of copyright protection applicable
to the subject matter hereof or the duration until the Licensee ceases
its function or operations, whichever comes earlier.

2. Any sub-licenses granted by the Licensee under this Agreement shall
remain valid should the Licensee cease to exist as a legal entity.


	 ARTICLE 5 - TECHNICAL SUPPORT AND FURTHER ASSURANCE

1. The provision of any Technical Support by the Licensor will be
agreed mutually on a case by case basis and in writing.

2. The Parties shall execute such further documents and do such
further things as may be necessary to implement and carry out the
intent of this License Agreement or to establish, perfect, preserve or
enforce rights granted under this License Agreement.


	     ARTICLE 6 - INDEMNIFICATION AND LIABILITIES

1. The Source Code, the Software and the Software Package are provided
"as is" without warranty of any kind, either express or implied,
including, but not limited to, any warranty against infringement of
third parties' property rights, of merchantability and fitness for a
particular purpose.

2. The entire risk as to the Use, quality, and performance of the
Source Code, the Software or the Software Package is with the
Licensee. The Licensor will not be liable for any incidental,
consequential, direct or indirect damages including but not limited to
the loss of data, loss of profits, or any other financial loss arising
from the Use, or inability to Use, of the Source Code or the Software.

3. The Licensee shall defend at its own expense any claim, suit or
proceeding brought by any third party, insofar as such claim, suit or
proceedings arise from the Licensee?s Use of the Software or of any
Derivative Works. The Licensor shall, at Licensee?s request, give
the Licensee such assistance as it shall reasonably require in respect
of the conduct of such defence.


		     ARTICLE 7 - CONFIDENTIALITY

1. Subject to the secrecy obligation are all materials, drawings,
data, articles, items, or other technical information, which the
Licensor has marked as "confidential" and has provided to the
Licensee and have not already been made public by the Licensor. Verbal
explanations regarding the Source Code also fall under this
obligation.

2. The Licensee shall keep all confidential material and information
as confidential and may not disclose, make public, or pass on such
material or information to third parties except as required by law
and/ or regulatory bodies. The Licensee guarantees that all its
employees, consultants and contractors are bound by a confidentiality
agreement covering the above secrecy obligation.

3. Upon termination of this Agreement, all confidential material
provided to the Licensee shall be returned to the Licensor, and the
Licensee agrees to continue to keep the information, know-how, and
trade secrets contained therein confidential to the extent such
information has not already been made public by the Licensor.

4. The Licensor agrees to keep confidential all confidential
information of the Licensee and to provide the necessary means to
prevent unauthorised disclosure of such confidential information.


	ARTICLE 8 - APPLICABLE LAW AND SETTLEMENT OF DISPUTES

1. The ITER Organization is governed by an Agreement on Establishing
of the ITER International Fusion Energy Organization for the Joint
Implementation of the ITER Project and its annexes signed on 21
November 2004 and the ITER Agreement on privileges and immunities
signed on the same day. The applicable law for the License Agreements
interpretation is French law.

2. In the event of any disputes arising out of or in connection with
the present Agreement, the Parties agree to submit the matter to
settlement proceedings under the International Chamber of Commerce in
Paris dispute settlement mediation ADR Rules. If the dispute has not
been settled pursuant to the said Rules within 45 days following the
filing of a Request for ADR or within such other period as the Parties
may agree in writing, such dispute shall be finally settled under the
Rules of Arbitration of the International Chamber of Commerce in Paris
by one or more arbitrators appointed in accordance with the said Rules
of Arbitration.

3. The arbitration proceedings in English shall take place in Paris
unless otherwise agreed by the Parties.


		ARTICLE 9 - PRIVILEGES AND IMMUNITIES

This Agreement shall not constitute a renunciation on the part of the
Licensee to its privileges and immunities pursuant to the ITER
Agreement on privileges and immunities of 21 November 2006.


